.\" dired-ji3
.\" dired-ta2
.\" Manual page for dired, a terminal directory editor.
.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt DIRED 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dired
.Nd terminal directory editor with the Emacs Dired interaction model
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar path
.Nm
.Fl \-check\-config
.Nm
.Fl h | \-help
.Nm
.Fl V | \-version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows a directory as a buffer of lines, one per entry.
A cursor, called
.Em point ,
moves between lines.
Entries are
.Em marked
to select them, and commands then act on every marked entry at once:
copy, move, rename, change mode, delete, or run a program.
When nothing is marked, a command acts on the entry at point.
.Pp
Every destructive operation is first shown in a
.Em preview buffer
that lists each source, destination, and problem, and nothing changes
until the preview is confirmed.
Deletion moves files to the system trash by default.
Completed operations are recorded in a journal and can usually be undone.
.Pp
If
.Ar path
is a directory,
.Nm
opens it.
If it is a file, its directory is opened with point on the file.
The default is the current working directory.
.Pp
The screen can be divided into
.Em windows ,
each showing a buffer with its own point, as in Emacs; see
.Sx WINDOWS .
.Pp
Large directories are listed immediately: names appear first and
metadata (size, time, mode) is filled in asynchronously.
The interface never blocks while directories are read, operations run,
or programs execute.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-check\-config
Load the configuration file, print every problem found, and exit.
The exit status is 0 if the configuration is clean, 1 otherwise.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl \-version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh KEY NOTATION
Keys are written in Emacs notation.
.Cm C\-x
means Control and
.Cm x
together.
.Cm M\-x
means Meta (Alt or Option) and
.Cm x ;
pressing
.Cm ESC
followed by
.Cm x
is equivalent.
.Cm C\-x d
is the two-key sequence
.Cm C\-x
followed by
.Cm d .
.Cm RET ,
.Cm TAB ,
.Cm DEL ,
.Cm SPC ,
and
.Cm ESC
are Return, Tab, Backspace, Space, and Escape.
Uppercase letters are distinct commands.
.Pp
While a prefix key such as
.Cm C\-x
or
.Cm %
is pending, the status line lists the keys that may follow it.
.Sh THE DISPLAY
Each entry line begins with three state columns:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It Sy >
The line is point.
.It Sy *
The entry is marked.
.It Sy D
The entry is flagged for deletion.
.It Sy \&!
The last operation on the entry failed; the error follows the name.
.It Sy ~
The name has been edited and not yet committed (Wdired mode).
.El
.Pp
These glyphs are configurable, and state is never shown by color alone.
They are followed by the mode, size, modification time, and name.
Inside a git repository a two-letter status column
(index state, then worktree state, as in
.Ql git status \-s )
precedes the mode; directories summarize the changes inside them, and the
mode line shows the branch, for example
.Ql git:main*\(ua1 .
On narrow terminals the mode, time, size, and git columns are dropped, in
that order, before names are truncated.
A suffix after the name gives the type:
.Sq /
directory,
.Sq @
symbolic link (followed by
.Sq -> Ar target ) ,
.Sq @/
link to a directory,
.Sq *
executable,
.Sq |
FIFO,
.Sq =
socket.
The first line,
.Sq .. ,
is the parent directory and cannot be marked or modified.
.Pp
The status line shows the buffer name, the number of marked and
flagged entries, the scan state
.Po Sq scanning Ar n ,
.Sq stat Ar n Ns / Ns Ar total ,
.Sq done ,
or an error
.Pc ,
the active filter, and the sort order.
Each window has its own status line (mode line) below it; the selected
window's is highlighted.
The last line of the screen is the echo area, which shows messages and
becomes the minibuffer when a command prompts for input.
.Sh MARKS AND OPERANDS
Batch commands operate on the
.Em visible
marked entries of the current buffer; if none are marked, on the entry
at point.
Deletion is the exception: it acts on every flagged entry in the buffer,
including hidden and filtered-out ones.
.Pp
A line carries exactly one state: unmarked, marked, or flagged.
Marks are attached to the entry's identity, not its position, and
survive refreshing, re-sorting, filtering, hiding, and renaming.
Each directory buffer has its own marks, point, sort order, filter,
hidden-file setting, and inserted subdirectories.
.Sh COMMANDS
The command name is shown in parentheses; every command can also be run
with
.Cm M\-x .
.Ss Motion
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm C\-n , Cm n , Cm SPC , Cm <down>
Next line
.Pq Ic next\-line .
.It Cm C\-p , Cm p , Cm <up>
Previous line
.Pq Ic previous\-line .
.It Cm M\-< , Cm <home>
First line
.Pq Ic beginning\-of\-buffer .
.It Cm M\-> , Cm <end>
Last line
.Pq Ic end\-of\-buffer .
.It Cm C\-v , Cm <next>
Forward one screen
.Pq Ic scroll\-up\-command .
.It Cm M\-v , Cm <prior>
Back one screen
.Pq Ic scroll\-down\-command .
.It Cm C\-s , Cm C\-r
Incremental search forward or backward
.Pq Ic isearch\-forward , Ic isearch\-backward .
Point moves to the first name containing the text as it is typed.
Case is ignored unless the text contains an uppercase letter.
Within the search,
.Cm C\-s
and
.Cm C\-r
find the next and previous match,
.Cm RET
stays, and
.Cm C\-g
returns to where the search began.
.It Cm j
Prompt for a substring and move to the next entry whose name contains
it, ignoring case
.Pq Ic dired\-goto\-file .
.El
.Ss Visiting
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm RET , Cm f , Cm e
Visit the entry at point
.Pq Ic dired\-visit .
Directories open in a directory buffer, or an existing buffer for that
directory is selected.
Files are passed to an opener; see
.Sx OPENERS .
.It Cm O
Choose a program to open the operands (the marked entries, else the entry
at point) with
.Pq Ic dired\-open\-with .
The candidates are the configured
.Cm [[open_with]]
entries followed by the built-in
.Dq System default
and
.Dq $EDITOR ;
see
.Sx OPENERS .
.It Cm C\-c t
Open a new terminal window in the directory of the buffer
.Pq Ic dired\-open\-terminal .
The command comes from
.Cm [terminal] ;
see
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
.It Cm C\-c s
Suspend the interface and run
.Ev SHELL
(else
.Pa /bin/sh )
in the directory of the buffer
.Pq Ic dired\-shell\-here .
Exit the shell to return.
.It Cm J
Pick a bookmark or a frequently used directory and open it
.Pq Ic jump\-to\-directory .
Bookmarks come first, then directories ranked by how often and how recently
they were opened; typing narrows the list as in
.Ic M\-x .
.It Cm C\-x 4 J
Like
.Cm J ,
but open the chosen directory in the other window, splitting first if there is
only one
.Pq Ic jump\-to\-directory\-other\-window .
.It Cm C\-x r m , Cm C\-x r d
Bookmark the current directory under a name
.Pq Ic dired\-bookmark\-set ,
or delete a bookmark
.Pq Ic dired\-bookmark\-delete .
.It Cm ^
Visit the parent directory with point on the current one
.Pq Ic dired\-up\-directory .
.It Cm o
Visit the directory at point (or
.Sq .. )
in the other window; files are opened as with
.Cm RET
.Pq Ic dired\-find\-file\-other\-window .
.It Cm g
Reread the directory asynchronously, keeping point on the same entry, and
ask git for its status again
.Pq Ic revert\-buffer .
This also happens automatically, for every directory on screen, when a
program that dired suspended itself for (an editor, a pager) exits.
.It Cm C\-x d
Prompt for a directory and open it
.Pq Ic dired\-open\-directory .
.It Cm C\-x C\-f
Prompt for a path; directories are opened and files visited
.Pq Ic dired\-find\-file .
.El
.Ss Marking
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm m
Mark the entry at point and move down
.Pq Ic dired\-mark .
.It Cm u
Remove the mark or deletion flag (and any error annotation) at point and
move down
.Pq Ic dired\-unmark .
.It Cm DEL
Move up and unmark that line
.Pq Ic dired\-unmark\-backward .
.It Cm U
Remove every ordinary mark in the buffer; deletion flags are kept
.Pq Ic dired\-unmark\-all\-marks .
.It Cm t
Toggle marks on all visible entries; flagged entries are unchanged
.Pq Ic dired\-toggle\-marks .
.It Cm d
Flag the entry at point for deletion and move down
.Pq Ic dired\-flag\-file\-deletion .
.It Cm % m
Mark entries whose names match a regular expression
.Pq Ic dired\-mark\-files\-regexp .
.It Cm % u
Unmark entries whose names match a regular expression
.Pq Ic dired\-unmark\-files\-regexp .
.It Cm % d
Flag entries whose names match a regular expression
.Pq Ic dired\-flag\-files\-regexp .
.It Cm % M , Cm % D , Cm % U
Mark, flag for deletion, or unmark entries satisfying a condition
.Pq Ic dired\-mark\-files\-where , dired\-flag\-files\-where , dired\-unmark\-files\-where .
A condition is one or more space-separated terms that must all hold:
.Cm size
.Ar op Ar n
(units B K M G T),
.Cm age
.Ar op Ar n
(units s m h d w y; time since modification),
.Cm type Ns = Ns Ar file|dir|link|exec ,
.Cm ext Ns = Ns Ar a,b ,
and
.Cm name Ns ~ Ns Ar regexp .
Operators are
.Li > ,
.Li < ,
.Li >= ,
.Li <= ,
.Li =
and
.Li != .
For example
.Li type=file ext=log age>30d .
Directories never match
.Cm size
terms; entries whose metadata has not loaded yet are skipped and counted.
.El
.Pp
The
.Cm %
commands test the regular expression against each visible entry's file
name only, never its directory.
The match is unanchored and case-sensitive; use
.Sq ^
and
.Sq $
to anchor and a leading
.Sq (?i)
to ignore case.
Existing marks are kept, and a matching flagged entry becomes marked.
The syntax is that of the Rust
.Sy regex
crate; see
.Sx REGULAR EXPRESSIONS .
.Ss File operations
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm C
Copy the operands
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-copy .
.It Cm R
Move or rename the operands
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-rename .
.It Cm M
Change the mode of the operands to an octal value such as
.Li 644
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-chmod .
.It Cm w , Cm W
Copy the file names
.Pq Ic dired\-copy\-name
or full paths
.Pq Ic dired\-copy\-path
of the operands to the clipboard, one per line.
The terminal's OSC 52 escape is always sent; outside an ssh session the text
is also given to
.Xr pbcopy 1 ,
.Xr wl\-copy 1 ,
.Xr xclip 1
or
.Xr xsel 1
when one is installed.
.It Cm +
Create a directory
.Pq Ic dired\-create\-directory .
This is the only operation performed without a preview.
.It Cm x
Preview and delete all flagged entries
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-flagged\-delete .
.It Cm % r , Cm % R
Rename the operands by regular-expression replacement
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-rename\-regexp .
.It Cm C\-x C\-q
Edit file names as text (Wdired)
.Pq Ic wdired\-toggle\-edit .
.It Cm C\-x u , Cm C\-/ , Cm C\-_
Undo the most recent reversible operation
.Pq Ic dired\-undo .
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm C
and
.Cm R
the destination prompt starts at the directory shown in another window,
if one shows a different directory, and otherwise at the current
directory; see
.Sx WINDOWS .
Relative paths are resolved against the current directory.
An existing directory receives the operands under their own names.
With a single operand, a path that does not exist becomes the new name.
Several operands require an existing directory.
Directories are copied recursively; symbolic links inside them are
copied as links and never followed.
.Pp
A move between filesystems is performed as copy followed by delete; the
preview marks it non-atomic, and the source is deleted only after its
copy has completed.
.Ss Programs and search
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm \&!
Run a program on the operands
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-shell\-command ;
see
.Sx RUNNING PROGRAMS .
Output is collected in the
.Sq *Shell Command Output*
buffer.
.It Cm &
Like
.Cm \&! ,
but switch to an
.Sq *Async Shell Command*
buffer that shows output as it arrives
.Pq Ic dired\-do\-async\-shell\-command .
.It Cm A
Search file contents with
.Xr rg 1
.Pq Ic dired\-search\-files\-content .
The marked entries are searched, or the whole directory tree if none
are marked.
Matches are listed in a search buffer;
.Cm RET
on a match visits the file at that line.
.El
.Ss Inserted subdirectories
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm i
Insert the directory at point below its line as an indented subtree
.Pq Ic dired\-maybe\-insert\-subdir .
Entries in the subtree can be marked and operated on like any other.
Symbolic links to directories are not inserted unless
.Cm follow_symlinks_on_insert
is set, and never when they lead back to an ancestor.
.It Cm $
Collapse the inserted subtree at point, or the one containing point
.Pq Ic dired\-hide\-subdir .
.El
.Ss View
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm s
Cycle the sort order: name, natural, time (newest first), size (largest
first), extension
.Pq Ic dired\-sort\-toggle\-or\-edit .
Directories are listed first unless changed with
.Ic dired\-sort\-toggle\-dirs\-first .
.It Cm /
Show only entries whose names contain a substring, ignoring case; an
empty string removes the filter
.Pq Ic dired\-filter .
.It Cm C\-x M\-o
Show or hide dot files
.Pq Ic dired\-toggle\-hidden .
.El
.Ss Buffers and general
.Bl -tag -width "C-x C-f" -compact
.It Cm b , Cm C\-x b
Switch buffer with fuzzy completion
.Pq Ic switch\-to\-buffer .
.It Cm q
Close the current buffer; closing the last one exits
.Pq Ic quit\-window .
In a preview, return to the directory without changes.
.It Cm C\-x k
Close a named buffer (default: the current one), asking first if it has
uncommitted Wdired edits, a pending or running operation, or a running
program
.Pq Ic buffer\-kill .
.It Cm \&? , Cm h , Cm C\-h m , Cm C\-h b , Cm <f1>
Show every key binding and command
.Pq Ic help\-for\-help .
.It Cm M\-x
Run a command by name with fuzzy completion
.Pq Ic execute\-extended\-command .
.It Cm C\-g
Cancel
.Pq Ic keyboard\-quit ;
see
.Sx CANCELLING .
.It Cm C\-x C\-c
Exit
.Pq Ic save\-buffers\-kill\-terminal .
.El
.Ss Commands without default keys
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ic dired\-do\-flagged\-delete\-permanently
Delete flagged entries without the trash.
.It Ic dired\-do\-shell\-command\-in\-shell
Run a command through the shell; see
.Sx RUNNING PROGRAMS .
.It Ic dired\-sort\-by\-name , Ic dired\-sort\-by\-natural , Ic dired\-sort\-by\-time , Ic dired\-sort\-by\-size , Ic dired\-sort\-by\-extension
Select a sort order.
.It Ic dired\-sort\-reverse
Reverse the sort order.
.It Ic dired\-sort\-toggle\-dirs\-first
List directories first or mixed with files.
.It Ic cycle\-theme
Switch to the next built-in theme for this session.
.It Ic dired\-reload\-config
Reread the configuration file.
.It Ic view\-diagnostics , Ic view\-messages
Show configuration problems or the message history.
.El
.Sh WINDOWS
The screen is divided into one or more windows, each showing a buffer
and followed by its mode line.
Side-by-side windows are separated by a vertical rule.
Keys act on the
.Em selected
window, whose mode line is highlighted; only it highlights the point
line and shows the cursor, while other windows mark point with the
focus glyph.
.Bl -tag -width "C-x 4 f, C-x 4 C-f" -compact
.It Cm C\-x 2
Split the selected window into two, one above the other
.Pq Ic split\-window\-below .
.It Cm C\-x 3
Split the selected window into two, side by side
.Pq Ic split\-window\-right .
.It Cm C\-x o
Select the next window in layout order
.Pq Ic other\-window .
.It Cm C\-x 0
Close the selected window; its buffer stays open
.Pq Ic delete\-window .
The last window cannot be closed.
.It Cm C\-x 1
Close every other window
.Pq Ic delete\-other\-windows .
.It Cm C\-x 4 d
Prompt for a directory and open it in the other window
.Pq Ic dired\-other\-window .
.It Cm C\-x 4 f , Cm C\-x 4 C\-f
Prompt for a path and open it in the other window
.Pq Ic find\-file\-other\-window .
.It Cm C\-x 4 b
Show a buffer in the other window
.Pq Ic switch\-to\-buffer\-other\-window .
.It Cm o
In a directory buffer, visit the directory at point in the other window
.Pq Ic dired\-find\-file\-other\-window .
.It Cm v
In a directory buffer, show the file at point in a read-only view window
.Pq Ic split\-window\-to\-file\-view .
The view uses the window already showing it, else the other window (splitting
if there is only one); point stays in the directory window.
Text files show a header and up to 500 lines or 64 KiB, binary files a hex
dump of the first 256 bytes, directories up to 200 entries.
Pipes, sockets and devices are never opened.
Pressing
.Cm v
again refreshes the view; it does not follow point.
.It Cm C\-M\-v , Cm C\-M\-b
Scroll the other window (preferring the view window) forward or back one page
.Pq Ic scroll\-other\-window , scroll\-other\-window\-down .
.El
.Pp
The
.Em other window
is the next window after the selected one.
When there is only one window, the commands above first split it: side
by side if it is at least 100 columns wide or too short to stack,
otherwise one above the other.
They then select the other window.
.Cm C\-x 4 f
on a file opens the file without splitting.
.Pp
Point and scroll position belong to the window: a directory shown in two
windows has two independent points.
Marks, flags, sort order, filter, hidden-file setting, inserted
subdirectories, and Wdired edits belong to the buffer and are shared by
all windows showing it.
A prompt acts on the window it was started from.
.Pp
When another window shows a different directory,
.Cm C
and
.Cm R
offer that directory as the destination, like Emacs's
.Va dired\-dwim\-target ;
the next such window in
.Cm C\-x o
order is used.
Set
.Cm dwim_target
to false in
.Cm [display]
to always start from the current directory.
.Pp
Previews open in a new window below the window showing the directory
they came from, and that window closes when the preview is closed; if it
is too small to split, the preview replaces it.
A preview that becomes ready while a prompt is open waits in the
background.
Closing a buffer closes a window that was popped up for it; other windows
showing it switch to the most recently used buffer not already visible.
Windows are split in half, and a split that would leave a window smaller
than 4 rows (including its mode line) or 20 columns is refused.
.Sh PREVIEWS AND CONFLICTS
Copy, move, rename, change-mode, and delete operations open a preview
buffer, in a window below the current one, listing each item as
.Ar source No \(-> Ar destination
with its state.
While any conflict is undecided, the operation cannot run.
.Bl -tag -width "x, y, C-c C-c" -offset indent
.It Cm x , Cm y , Cm C\-c C\-c
Execute.
.It Cm q , Cm C\-g
Cancel without changes.
.It Cm n , Cm p
Move between items.
.It Cm o
Overwrite the existing destination of this item.
.It Cm s
Skip this item.
.It Cm r
Prompt for a different destination for this item.
.It Cm a
Mark this item undecided again.
.It Cm O , Cm S
Overwrite or skip every undecided conflict where that is permitted.
.It Cm D
In a deletion preview, switch between trash and permanent deletion.
.El
.Pp
The conflicts detected, and the resolutions each permits, are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It destination exists
overwrite, skip, or rename.
.It duplicate destination
Two sources map to one destination: skip or rename.
.It destination is inside source
Copying or moving a directory into itself, including through symbolic
links: skip.
.It destination contains the source
Replacing an ancestor of the source: skip.
.It source and destination are the same
skip.
.It inaccessible
The source cannot be read: skip.
.El
.Pp
Overwriting requires typing
.Li yes
at a prompt stating how many destinations, and how many directories, will
be replaced.
The existing destination is renamed aside first, removed only after the
new data is in place, and restored if the operation fails.
.Pp
After execution the preview shows each item as done, skipped, or failed
with the reason.
Failures do not stop unrelated items.
.Sh DELETION
.Cm d
and
.Cm % d
only flag entries.
.Cm x
opens a preview of every flagged entry with the resolved backend and a
count; confirming it moves the entries to the system trash (the macOS
Trash, the freedesktop.org trash, or the Windows Recycle Bin).
Directories are trashed with their contents.
.Pp
Permanent deletion, selected with
.Cm D
in the preview or with
.Ic dired\-do\-flagged\-delete\-permanently ,
requires typing
.Li yes
at a prompt stating the number of items and of directories removed
recursively.
Symbolic links are removed, never followed.
If no trash is available the preview refuses to run unless
.Cm trash_fallback
is
.Qq permanent .
.Pp
Entries that are deleted disappear from the buffer.
Entries that fail keep their flag and show the error.
.Sh RENAMING
.Ss Wdired
.Cm C\-x C\-q
makes file names editable in place.
Only the name portion of each line can change; metadata, state columns,
.Sq .. ,
and names that cannot be represented exactly as text are read-only.
Printing characters insert at the cursor;
.Cm DEL ,
.Cm C\-d ,
.Cm C\-k ,
.Cm C\-a ,
.Cm C\-e ,
.Cm C\-f ,
.Cm C\-b ,
and the arrow keys edit and move within the name, and
.Cm C\-n
and
.Cm C\-p
move between lines.
.Pp
.Cm C\-c C\-c
(or
.Cm C\-x C\-q ,
.Cm C\-x C\-s )
validates the complete set of renames and opens a preview.
Empty names,
.Sq \&. ,
.Sq .. ,
names containing
.Sq / ,
two entries renamed to the same name, and a new name equal to an
existing entry that is not itself renamed are reported and nothing is
changed; editing continues.
Cycles and chains such as
.Sy a No \(-> Sy b , Sy b No \(-> Sy a
are executed through temporary names in the same directory.
Marks and flags follow renamed entries.
.Cm C\-c C\-k
discards all edits.
.Ss Regular-expression rename
.Cm % r
prompts for a regular expression and a replacement, and renames each
operand whose name matches by replacing the first match.
In the replacement,
.Li $0
is the whole match,
.Li $1 , $2 , ...
are numbered groups,
.Li $name
is a named group, and
.Li ${1}
separates a group reference from following text.
For example, from
.Li ^IMG_(\ed+)
to
.Li photo\-$1
renames
.Pa IMG_0042.JPG
to
.Pa photo\-0042.JPG .
.Pp
Batch renames are all or nothing: items cannot be skipped individually.
.Sh RUNNING PROGRAMS
.Cm \&!
and
.Cm &
split the command into words using shell-like quoting and run it
directly with the operands as separate arguments, from the buffer's
directory.
No shell is involved, so file names are never interpreted and pipes,
redirections, and variables in the command are passed literally.
Operands are given relative to the directory; a name beginning with
.Sq \-
is passed as
.Sq ./ Ns Ar name .
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It A word consisting of Sq *
is replaced by all operands, and the program runs once:
.Li tar czf out.tgz *
.It A word consisting of Sq \&?
runs the program once per operand, with the word replaced by it:
.Li gzip \-k \&?
.It Otherwise
the operands are appended:
.Li wc \-l
.El
.Pp
.Ic dired\-do\-shell\-command\-in\-shell
runs the command with
.Ql /bin/sh \-c
(see
.Cm shell
in
.Sx CONFIGURATION )
after the user types
.Li yes .
The operands are still passed as positional parameters, available as
.Li \&"$@\&"
(a
.Sq *
word is replaced by it) or
.Li \&"$1\&"
(a
.Sq \&?
word), and are never inserted into the command text.
Output buffers are labeled
.Sq [SHELL MODE] .
.Pp
In an output buffer
.Cm C\-c C\-c
or
.Cm C\-g
stops the program.
.Sh MINIBUFFER
.Bl -tag -width "M-p, M-n" -compact
.It Cm RET
Accept.
.It Cm C\-g
Cancel.
.It Cm TAB
Complete: paths asynchronously to the longest common prefix, commands
and buffer names to the best fuzzy match.
.It Cm C\-n , Cm C\-p
Select the next or previous candidate in prompts that list candidates
.Pq Ic M\-x , buffer switching, open-with, theme names, and path prompts once
.Cm TAB
has listed the matches ;
also
.Cm <down>
and
.Cm <up> .
.Cm RET
then accepts the highlighted candidate; in path prompts
.Cm TAB
inserts it and completes further.
.It Cm M\-p , Cm M\-n
Previous or next history entry; also
.Cm <up>
and
.Cm <down>
in prompts without a candidate list.
.It Cm C\-a , Cm C\-e
Beginning or end of line.
.It Cm C\-f , Cm C\-b
Forward or back one character.
.It Cm C\-d , Cm DEL
Delete forward or backward.
.It Cm C\-k , Cm C\-u
Delete to end or beginning of line.
.It Cm C\-w , Cm M\-DEL
Delete the previous word or path component.
.El
.Pp
History is kept per kind of prompt and persists across sessions unless
.Cm persist_history
is false.
.Pp
In path prompts, which are usually pre-filled, typing
.Sq ~/
or a second
.Sq /
starts a new path, as in Emacs:
.Pa /src/~/backup
means
.Pa ~/backup
and
.Pa /src//tmp
means
.Pa /tmp .
.Sh CANCELLING
.Cm C\-g
cancels the first of the following that applies: the open prompt
(an incremental search also restores point); a preview that has not run,
which closes with nothing changed; a partially typed key sequence; a
running task in the current buffer (a directory scan, a program, or the
remaining items of an operation).
.Sh UNDO
.Ic dired\-undo
reverses the most recent journaled operation that has not been undone:
moves and renames are renamed back, copies are moved to the trash
(unless they overwrote something), created directories are removed if
empty, and modes are restored.
Trashed files must be restored from the system trash; permanent
deletions cannot be undone.
Undo is best effort and reports steps that fail.
.Sh REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Regular expressions follow the syntax of the Rust
.Sy regex
crate: no backreferences or look-around.
Commonly used constructs:
.Bl -tag -width "[^abc]" -compact -offset indent
.It Li \&.
any character
.It Li \e.
a literal dot; precede any special character with
.Li \e
to match it literally
.It Li ^ , $
start and end of the name
.It Li * , + , \&?
zero or more, one or more, zero or one of the preceding item
.It Li {n,m}
between n and m repetitions
.It Li [abc] , [^abc]
one of, or none of, the listed characters
.It Li \ed , \ew , \es
digit, word character, white space
.It Li a|b
either alternative
.It Li (...)
group, referenced as
.Li $1
in replacements
.It Li (?i)
at the start: ignore case
.El
.Pp
Shell globs are not regular expressions: write
.Li \e.txt$
rather than
.Li *.txt ,
and
.Li ^IMG_
rather than
.Li IMG_* .
.Sh OPENERS
Visiting a file uses the first
.Cm [[opener]]
rule whose
.Cm extensions
include the file's extension.
Without a matching rule,
.Ev VISUAL
or
.Ev EDITOR
is run in the terminal, with
.Ar +line
when visiting a search match; otherwise
.Xr open 1
on macOS or
.Xr xdg\-open 1
elsewhere.
While a program runs in the terminal the interface is suspended.
.Pp
.Cm O
instead asks which program to use for the operands, completing over the
.Cm [[open_with]]
entries (then
.Dq System default
and, when set,
.Dq $EDITOR ) .
An entry with
.Cm extensions
is offered only when every operand matches.
A command containing
.Li {files}
runs once with all operands; one with only
.Li {file}
runs once per operand.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is TOML.
Errors never prevent
.Nm
from starting; each problem is listed in the
.Sq *Diagnostics*
buffer and the affected setting keeps its default.
A commented example is installed as
.Pa share/dired/examples/config.toml
under the installation prefix.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm version No = Ar 1
Configuration format version.
.It Cm [display]
.Bl -tag -width "show_hidden" -compact
.It Cm show_hidden
Show dot files (default true).
.It Cm dirs_first
List directories first (default true).
.It Cm sort
.Qq name ,
.Qq natural ,
.Qq time ,
.Qq size ,
or
.Qq extension .
.It Cm columns
Metadata columns in order, from
.Qq git ,
.Qq permissions ,
.Qq size ,
and
.Qq mtime
(default: all four; the git column only appears inside a repository).
.It Cm dwim_target
Offer the directory in another window as the copy and move destination
(default true).
.It Cm style
The look of the interface:
.Cm rich
(framed windows, icons),
.Cm clean
(default) or
.Cm minimal .
.Ic cycle\-style
steps through them and
.Ic load\-style
asks for one by name, both for the session.
.It Cm [display.ui]
Per-feature overrides of the style:
.Cm borders ,
.Cm icons ,
.Cm scrollbar ,
.Cm column_colors
and
.Cm popup
(booleans), and
.Cm mode_line
.Pq Qq segmented No or Qq plain .
.El
.It Cm [theme]
.Bl -tag -width "show_hidden" -compact
.It Cm name
.Qq default ,
.Qq high-contrast ,
.Qq mono ,
.Qq nord ,
.Qq gruvbox ,
.Qq solarized-dark ,
or
.Qq dracula .
.It Cm [theme.colors]
.Ar role No = Qq Ar color
sets the foreground of a role.
.It Cm [theme.styles.\& Ns Ar role Ns Cm \&]
Keys
.Cm fg ,
.Cm bg ,
.Cm bold ,
.Cm italic ,
.Cm underline ,
.Cm reversed ,
.Cm dim .
.It Cm [theme.glyphs]
Keys
.Cm focused ,
.Cm marked ,
.Cm flagged ,
.Cm error ,
.Cm changed .
.El
Roles are
normal, directory, symlink, executable, special, marked, flagged, error,
changed, header, dim, warning, conflict, success, matched, status,
point, prompt, and inactive (the mode line of unselected windows).
Colors are ANSI names such as
.Qq lightblue
or
.Qq # Ns Ar rrggbb ;
hexadecimal colors are approximated by the nearest ANSI color unless
.Ev COLORTERM
indicates truecolor.
.It Cm [[keymap]]
Each entry has
.Cm key
(a key sequence in the notation above),
optionally
.Cm mode
.Po
.Qq global ,
.Qq dired
(default),
.Qq wdired ,
.Qq preview ,
or
.Qq text
.Pc ,
and exactly one of:
.Bl -tag -width "command" -compact
.It Cm command
the name of a command.
.It Cm exec
an argument vector; the word
.Li {file}
runs the program once per operand, and
.Li {files}
expands to all operands.
.It Cm plugin
reserved for future use.
.El
Unknown commands and duplicate key sequences are reported.
.It Cm [[opener]]
.Cm extensions
(list, case-insensitive; empty matches everything),
.Cm command
(argument vector with
.Li {file}
and
.Li {line} ) ,
and
.Cm terminal
(run in the terminal instead of detached).
.It Cm [[open_with]]
.Cm name
(label shown in the picker),
.Cm command
(argument vector with
.Li {file} ) ,
.Cm extensions
(list, case-insensitive; empty offers it for every entry),
and
.Cm terminal
(run in the terminal instead of detached).
.It Cm [git]
.Cm enabled :
ask git for status in the background (default true).
Git runs with
.Ev GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS Ns =0
and is abandoned after 10 seconds.
.It Cm [terminal]
.Cm command :
argument vector that opens a terminal window for
.Ic dired\-open\-terminal ;
.Ql {dir}
is replaced by the directory, and the program starts there.
Empty (the default) uses Terminal.app on macOS, else
.Ev TERMINAL
or
.Xr x-terminal-emulator 1 .
.It Cm [shell]
.Cm shell :
the interpreter for shell mode (default
.Pa /bin/sh ) .
.It Cm [search]
.Cm command :
argument vector for
.Cm A ,
to which the pattern and search roots are appended; it must produce
.Ql rg \-\-json
output.
Default
.Ql [\&"rg\&", \&"\-\-json\&", \&"\-e\&"] .
.It Cm [safety]
.Bl -tag -width "follow_symlinks_on_insert" -compact
.It Cm trash_fallback
.Qq refuse
(default) or
.Qq permanent .
.It Cm follow_symlinks_on_insert
Allow
.Cm i
on directory symlinks (default false).
.It Cm persist_history
Save minibuffer history (default true).
.El
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "XDG_CONFIG_HOME"
.It Ev DIRED_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base configuration directory when
.Ev DIRED_CONFIG
is unset.
.It Ev DIRED_STATE_DIR
Directory for history, the journal, and diagnostics.
.It Ev VISUAL , Ev EDITOR
Program used to visit files without a matching opener.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set, use the
.Qq mono
theme.
.It Ev COLORTERM
If it contains
.Qq truecolor
or
.Qq 24bit ,
hexadecimal colors are rendered exactly.
.It Ev HOME
Expansion of
.Sq ~
in paths.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dired/config.toml
Configuration; defaults to
.Pa ~/.config/dired/config.toml .
.It Pa history.json
Minibuffer history, in the state directory.
.It Pa jumps.json
Bookmarks and, unless
.Cm persist_history
is false, visit counts for the jump list, in the state directory.
.It Pa journal.jsonl
Operation journal, one JSON record per operation, in the state directory.
.It Pa diagnostics.log
Problems found in the last configuration load, in the state directory.
.El
.Pp
The state directory is
.Ev DIRED_STATE_DIR ,
or
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/dired
.Pq default Pa ~/.local/state/dired
on Linux and
.Pa ~/Library/Application Support/dired
on macOS.
.Nm
never uses the network.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
With
.Fl \-check\-config ,
the status is 1 if any problem was found.
An invalid option gives status 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Delete all log files, reviewing them first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% d  \e.log$  RET     flag matching entries
x                     preview
x                     move them to the trash
.Ed
.Pp
Move this year's photos into a new directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
+  2026  RET
% m  (?i)^IMG_2026.*\e.(jpe?g|heic)$  RET
R  2026/  RET
x
.Ed
.Pp
Rename camera files, then undo:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% m  ^DSC  RET
% r  ^DSC_(\ed+)  RET  holiday\-$1  RET
x
C\-x u
.Ed
.Pp
Swap two file names in Wdired:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
C\-x C\-q      edit a to b and b to a
C\-c C\-c      preview
x
.Ed
.Pp
Archive the marked files, and run a command once per file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
!  tar czf archive.tgz *  RET
!  gzip \-k ?  RET
.Ed
.Pp
Copy files between two directories shown side by side:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
C\-x 4 d  ~/backup  RET   open ~/backup in a second window
C\-x o                   back to the first window
m m                      mark two files
C  RET                   destination defaults to ~/backup/
x                        run the previewed copy
C\-x 1                   back to a single window
.Ed
.Pp
A configuration binding keys to a command and to an external program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
version = 1

[theme]
name = "nord"

[[keymap]]
key = "C\-c s"
command = "dired\-sort\-by\-size"

[[keymap]]
key = "C\-c d"
exec = ["du", "\-sh", "{files}"]
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr open 1 ,
.Xr rg 1 ,
.Xr xdg\-open 1
.Pp
The Emacs manual, node
.Dq Dired .
.Sh STANDARDS
The key bindings follow GNU Emacs Dired and Wdired where a standalone
equivalent exists.
There is no Vim mode.
.Sh CAVEATS
Undo is best effort and cannot restore data that an operation
overwrote or deleted permanently.
.Pp
Batch commands ignore marked entries that are hidden or filtered out;
the marked count in the status line includes them.
.Pp
Control characters in file names are displayed escaped (for example
.Sq \en
is shown for a newline), and the
.Cm %
commands match against the escaped form.
Such names cannot be edited in Wdired.
